{
  "id": "Compound::DB00761 - Gene::6557",
  "verbalization": "Potassium Chloride is implicated in the interaction with multiple Slc12 family members, including Slc12a1, through a series of molecular and functional pathways. The evidence suggests that Potassium Chloride binds to Slc12a6, Slc12a2, and Slc12a5, which are all involved in cation-coupled chloride transport. These transporters are part of a broader network of ion transport mechanisms that regulate chloride movement across cellular membranes. The presence of these interactions implies that Potassium Chloride may influence the activity of these transporters, potentially modulating chloride transport in response to changes in intracellular ion concentrations. The shared involvement of these transporters in the same functional context, such as sodium-potassium-chloride symporters, suggests a coordinated regulatory mechanism. While the exact functional relationship between Potassium Chloride and Slc12a1 remains to be fully elucidated, the evidence points to a role in the regulation of chloride transport through a network of related transporters.",
